{
  "verdict": "incorrect",
  "p_correct": 0.12,
  "errors": [
    "The problem gives no machine assignment, and the model invents one: both operations of each job go on the same private machine, and no machine is shared between jobs. This contradicts 'shared machines'. The only conflicts modelled are between a job's own two operations, which precedence already separates, so the machine constraints are vacuous.",
    "The resulting makespan of 2 is just the trivial lower bound from the precedence chain. A natural reading is a two-machine flow shop (first operations on one machine, second operations on the other), which forces the five operations on each machine to be sequenced and gives a makespan of about 6. The model's optimum would then be wrong."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "Machine assignment was fabricated as 5 machines with 2 operations each, one job per machine.",
    "Machine-sharing pairs were limited to the two operations of the same job."
  ],
  "reason": "The invented machine assignment removes all real machine contention between jobs, so the model reports a trivial makespan of 2 rather than the intended shared-machine schedule."
}

{
  "verdict": "incorrect",
  "p_correct": 0.02,
  "errors": [
    "The disjunctive machine constraints are incomplete. Each ordered pair (a,b) has only a one-way big-M constraint, and nothing forces precedes[a,b] + precedes[b,a] = 1. The solver can set both binaries to 0, which deactivates both constraints, so the no-overlap requirement is never enforced.",
    "The solution shows the effect: all five first operations start at time 0 and all five second operations start at time 1. On the single machine this model assumes, these operations overlap. Ten unit operations on one machine need a makespan of at least 10, not 2.",
    "The model puts every operation on one machine, but the text only says operations are on 'shared machines'. Whatever the true assignment is, the missing ordering constraint makes the reported optimum of 2 invalid."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "All 10 operations are assigned to a single machine, since the problem gives no machine assignment."
  ],
  "reason": "The big-M pair constraints lack the either-or link between the two ordering binaries, so machine overlap is never prevented and the makespan of 2 is invalid."
}
